<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Otto I of Nassau</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 1290</span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Otto I of Nassau was a 13th-century count of Nassau and founder of the Ottonian line of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-nassau">House of Nassau</a>. In the division of 1255 with his brother Walram II he received the lands north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herborn">Herborn</a>, while Walram kept the southern lands. His line later produced the counts of Nassau-Dillenburg and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-orange-nassau">House of Orange-Nassau</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Otto was born in 1224, the third son of Count Henry II of Nassau and Matilda of Guelders and Zutphen. Through his mother he descended from Count <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/otto-i">Otto I</a> of Guelders and Zutphen and from Otto I Wittelsbach, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. He is first mentioned in a charter of 1247. Before 1251 he and his brother Walram II succeeded their father, and in 1251 they received town privileges for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herborn">Herborn</a> from the German King William.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The brothers divided their county on 16 December 1255, with the river <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lahn">Lahn</a> as the border, in the treaty now known as the Prima divisio. Besides <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herborn">Herborn</a>, Otto received the lordships of Tringenstein, Neukirch and Emmerichenhain, part of the Kalenberger Zent, and Dietkirchen and Ems. Nassau Castle, Laurenburg Castle, the Esterau and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fief">fiefs</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hesse">Hesse</a> remained jointly owned.</p><p>Otto found it hard to enforce his rights at a time when the power of the supreme patron in the empire had sunk deeply. Disputes with the lords of Westerburg and the counts of Sayn over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westerwald">Westerwald</a>, and with the lords of Greifenstein and of Dernbach, often led to feuds whose course is unknown. In his struggles with the Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/trier">Trier</a> he lost the Vogtei of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/koblenz">Koblenz</a> and of Ems.</p><p>His dealings with Siegfried II of Westerburg, Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cologne">Cologne</a>, were mixed: on 8 April 1277 he joined lords in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westphalia">Westphalia</a> in a covenant to wage war on the archbishop, yet he was the archbishop's ally in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-limburg-succession">War of the Limburg Succession</a>. When he tried to reduce his father's rich gifts to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/teutonic-order">Teutonic Order</a>, he was named a robber of its goods in 1285 and excommunicated, and his county lay under interdict until the dispute was settled the following year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Otto founded the chapel in Feldbach before 1287. He last appears alive in a charter of 3 May 1289 and is named as dead in one of 19 March 1290; he was buried in Altenberg Abbey. With his wife Agnes of Leiningen, daughter of Count Emich IV of Leiningen, he had the sons Henry, Emicho and John, who succeeded him and in 1303 became counts of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/siegen">Siegen</a>, Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hadamar">Hadamar</a> and Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dillenburg">Dillenburg</a>. A daughter, Gertrudis, became Abbess of Altenberg Abbey.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Otto I of Nassau” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
